        exp: "Rule: Under UCC § 2-508(1), where any tender or delivery by the seller is rejected because non-conforming and the time for performance has not yet expired, the seller may seasonably notify the buyer of his intention to cure and may then within the contract time make a conforming delivery. The contractual delivery deadline was October 1. The initial tender and rejection occurred on September 15-16. The manufacturer gave prompt, seasonable notice of intention to cure on September 17, promising conforming delivery on October 1. The seller's statutory right to cure within the original contract window is absolute, overriding the buyer's attempt to cancel under the perfect tender rule (Option B). Option A is incorrect because the perfect tender rule (UCC § 2-601) is explicitly subject to the seller's right to cure under § 2-508. Option C is incorrect because a 'time is of the essence' clause requires performance by the specified date; it does not eliminate the right to cure prior to that date. Option D is incorrect because the perfect tender rule applies to sales of goods, but the right to cure prevents cancellation."
